    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bulimia nervosa, severe (F50.23)</content>. Marked improvement: two episodes in four weeks, from about ten a week at intake, and an EDE-Q global score of 2.1, from 3.9. Potassium 4.1 mmol/L and bicarbonate 25 mmol/L; amylase back to normal at 88 U/L as the parotid swelling settles. The dental examination is done. [S]<list><item>Continue CBT-E weekly to the end of the 20 sessions, then a written relapse-prevention plan.</item><item>Continue fluoxetine 60 mg each morning.</item><item>Dental review in six months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate (F33.1)</content>. PHQ-9 5, from 14. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the safety plan is current. [S]<list><item>PHQ-9 at the end-of-treatment visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Binge-eating and vomiting frequency (her self-monitoring record)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two episodes in the last four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Eating Disorder Examination Questionnaire (EDE-Q) global score</td><td>Baseline, mid-treatment, and end of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Global score 2.1 at mid-treatment, from 3.9</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit, and weekly in CBT-E sessions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>62.0 kg, BMI 19.6</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Lying and standing pulse and blood pressure</td><td>Every visit while purging continues</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Lying 108/68, pulse 68; standing 108/70, pulse 76 (rise 8)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium, chloride, bicarbonate, and creatinine</td><td>Weekly until potassium is normal twice, then at each medical visit while purging continues</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Potassium 4.1 mmol/L off supplements; sodium 139, chloride 102, bicarbonate 25 mmol/L; creatinine 0.79 mg/dL, eGFR 104</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Amylase</td><td>Baseline with parotid enlargement; repeat as indicated</td><td>Completed today</td><td>88 U/L, from 146 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>ECG with QTc</td><td>Baseline with purging or low potassium; after a QT-relevant medicine reaches its full dose while potassium has been low</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>QTc 441 ms on fluoxetine 60 mg with normal potassium; no further routine ECG unless symptoms, low potassium, or a new QT-relevant medicine</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Parotid and dental examination; dental protection</td><td>Every visit; a dental examination soon after diagnosis and regular dental reviews while vomiting continues</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dental examination done three weeks ago, about ten weeks after it was advised at intake; erosion stable, fluoride varnish, no new decay, review in six months. The delay is closed; no harm resulted</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Fluoxetine dose, adherence, and side effects (nausea, sleep, agitation, sexual effects, suicidal thoughts)</td><td>At the start, one to two weeks after starting or a dose change, then every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>60 mg; no side effects; no agitation or suicidal thoughts</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>CBT-E attendance and progress</td><td>Up to 20 sessions over 20 weeks, twice weekly for the first four weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sixteen sessions; weekly</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Regular eating and dietitian review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three meals and two snacks on six or seven days a week</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>Monthly during acute treatment, then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 5, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan, lethal means, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; safety plan current</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No binge eating or vomiting for at least four weeks by the end of 20 weeks of CBT-E. In progress; target 1990-07-13 [S]</item><item>Potassium at least 3.5 mmol/L on two checks within four weeks. Outcome by 1990-03-16: Potassium 3.6 and 3.9 mmol/L; supplement stopped [S]</item><item>Regular eating (three meals and two or three snacks) on most days by three months. Outcome by 1990-05-11: Regular eating on six or seven days a week [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1990-07-13 [S]</item><item>EDE-Q global score below 2.0 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1990-07-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Eating Disorders, Fourth Edition (2023); Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (NICE guideline NG69) (2017, updated 2020); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: CBT-E weekly to session 20. End-of-treatment review in about nine weeks with electrolytes, the EDE-Q, and the PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 1990-07-13: end-of-treatment review after 20 weeks of CBT-E: remission, relapse prevention, and continuing fluoxetine. [S]</paragraph></text>

